# Cavix on Oracle Cloud — every setting in one file.
#
# bootstrap.sh generates this with the random halves already filled in, so you
# normally edit the finished .env rather than copying this. It is here as the
# reference for what each value is and what happens when it is wrong.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env
#
# NEVER COMMIT .env. It holds the key to every stored API key on the deployment.

# ─────────────────────────── where the site lives ───────────────────────────

# The public hostname. Two choices:
#
#   A real domain      cavix.yourdomain.com, with an A record at the instance's
#                      public IP. Use this if you have one; it is the only option
#                      that survives you ever changing IP.
#
#   No domain          <ip-with-dashes>.sslip.io — for 152.67.12.34 that is
#                      152-67-12-34.sslip.io. sslip.io resolves any such name to
#                      that address, for free, with nothing to sign up for, and
#                      Let's Encrypt will issue for it.
#
# Whichever you pick, it must already resolve to this machine BEFORE the first
# `docker compose up`. Caddy asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate on startup and
# the validation is a request to this name on port 80; if it lands somewhere
# else, issuance fails and Caddy retries with a backoff that gets slow quickly.
CAVIX_DOMAIN=

# ───────────────────── secrets (bootstrap.sh generates) ─────────────────────

# THESE TWO MUST NEVER CHANGE ONCE THE SITE HAS USERS.
#
# CAVIX_SECRET_KEY encrypts every BYOK API key and every OAuth token in the
# database. Change it and none of those blobs decrypt any more — and the store
# treats a blob that will not decrypt as "no credential" rather than an error, so
# nothing fails loudly. The owner simply finds their key gone and their
# repositories disconnected, and is asked to set them up again.
#
# CAVIX_SESSION_SECRET signs login cookies. Change it and every session is
# invalidated at once.
#
# Rotating CAVIX_SECRET_KEY without re-encrypting is a data migration, not a
# config change. Keep a copy of both somewhere that is not this machine.
CAVIX_SESSION_SECRET=
CAVIX_SECRET_KEY=

# Lets the orchestrator read org keys from the control plane. Internal only.
CAVIX_INTERNAL_TOKEN=

# Paste this into your GitHub App's webhook secret field. The edge refuses to
# start without it, which is the correct failure: with no secret it cannot verify
# a delivery, and accepting unverified webhooks means anyone can queue reviews.
CAVIX_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Database and queue passwords. Neither is reachable from outside the box —
# nothing publishes 5432 or 6379 — so these guard against a compromised
# container, not against the internet. Hex on purpose: a password with @ or / in
# it has to be percent-encoded inside the connection URL, and that is a bug
# waiting to happen.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
REDIS_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────── who runs this thing ───────────────────────────

# YOUR email, or your GitHub login written @login → founder Admin console.
# Prefer @login: if your GitHub email is private, GitHub returns no address at
# sign-in and Cavix stores you under <login>@users.noreply.github.com, so an
# email here would match nothing and the console never appears.
# REQUIRED — left unset, nobody on this deployment is an admin.
CAVIX_ADMIN_EMAILS=

# ─────────────────────────── GitHub App (GUIDE §8B) ─────────────────────────

# ONE GitHub App powers sign-in AND bot installs.
#
# In the App's settings, with CAVIX_DOMAIN filled in above:
#   Callback URL  = https://<CAVIX_DOMAIN>/api/auth/github/callback
#   Setup URL     = https://<CAVIX_DOMAIN>/app     (Redirect on update: on)
#   Webhook URL   = https://<CAVIX_DOMAIN>/webhook
#   Webhook secret= CAVIX_WEBHOOK_SECRET above
#   Events        = Pull request AND Issue comment. Both. issue_comment is what
#                   delivers "@cavixcode review"; without it commands never
#                   arrive at all.
#   Permissions   = Pull requests: Read & write, Contents: Read, Metadata: Read
#                   (Checks: Read & write if you want the Checks box row)

# Client ID + a generated client secret, for sign-in. Leave blank to disable
# GitHub sign-in; email/password still works.
CAVIX_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
CAVIX_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The App's name in URL form, for the install button. From the App's public
# page: github.com/apps/<this>
CAVIX_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=

# The NUMERIC "App ID" on the App's General page — not the Client ID, which
# starts Iv1. or Iv23. Required for the orchestrator to post anything: an App
# install never hands you a token, so it signs a JWT with the key below and
# exchanges it for a short-lived installation token on every review.
CAVIX_APP_ID=

# The private key is a FILE, not a variable: put the .pem at
# deploy/oracle/secrets/github-app.pem. bootstrap.sh offers to do this for you.
# Nothing to set here.

# ─────────────────────────────── behaviour ─────────────────────────────────

# The handles Cavix answers to, comma-separated: "@cavixcode review".
CAVIX_BOT_HANDLE=cavixcode,cavix

# Default provider and model a new org sees. Each org brings its own key (BYOK).
CAVIX_LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic
CAVIX_LLM_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Where candidate tests run while Cavix is proving a finding.
#
#   local    child processes of the orchestrator container. No extra setup, and
#            the isolation is the container's — fine for reviewing your own
#            repositories, which is what a single-box deployment is for.
#
#   docker   a throwaway container per run, with --network=none. Stronger, and
#            the honest trade: it needs /var/run/docker.sock mounted into the
#            orchestrator, and access to that socket is root on this host. Only
#            worth it once you are reviewing code you do not control, and if you
#            get there, the answer is a second VM, not a mounted socket.
CAVIX_SANDBOX_BACKEND=local

# Let a re-review read only the files a push changed, instead of the whole pull
# request every time. Cheaper on long-running PRs; the trade is real, since a
# model asked a second time about untouched code might find something it missed.
CAVIX_NARROW_REREVIEWS=false

# ───────────────── other hosts (all optional, all off by default) ────────────

# Secrets for the non-GitHub hosts. They arrive on the SAME /webhook endpoint,
# each with its own secret so no host's hook can forge another's. Unset means
# that host's deliveries are refused, which is the default and the safe one.
CAVIX_GITLAB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
CAVIX_BITBUCKET_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Azure DevOps service hooks sign nothing, so the only credential they can carry
# is HTTP Basic. This is the PASSWORD half; put anything in the username box.
CAVIX_AZURE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Only needed to name a SELF-HOSTED instance. gitlab.com, bitbucket.org and
# dev.azure.com are the defaults and need nothing here.
CAVIX_GITLAB_URL=
CAVIX_BITBUCKET_SERVER_URL=
CAVIX_AZURE_URL=
